-- src/core/dst_hook_guard.ail
--
-- WI-C5. `routing_violation_at`'s PRODUCTION call site.
--
-- ============================================================================
-- Why this exists as a module rather than as a line in ext/runtime.ail
-- ============================================================================
--
-- D5: "An explicitly excluded hook is not covered, must be named in the result,
-- and causes a fail-closed HarnessFailure if dispatch reaches it."
--
-- `src/core/dst_profile.routing_violation_at` has been the check since WI-A10
-- and until now its only callers were its own self-tests and two acceptance
-- scripts. **An exclusion checked only at load time is an exclusion nothing
-- enforces at dispatch**, which is the gap this module closes.
--
-- It is a separate module because of a layering fact, not a preference:
-- `ExtRuntime` lives in `packages/motoko-ext-abi` and `ProfileDefinition` lives
-- in `src/core/dst_profile`. The ABI package cannot import core, so the runtime
-- record has nowhere to carry a profile and the guard cannot live inside the
-- folds themselves. It composes over them instead, which also keeps
-- `ext/runtime.ail` free of any DST dependency.
--
-- ============================================================================
-- WHY THE GATED SLOT, AND ONLY THE GATED SLOT
-- ============================================================================
--
-- Seven of the eight ABI slots are dispatched by an UNCONDITIONAL fold. D5's
-- load-time rules already reject a profile that excludes one of those, because
-- such a profile cannot complete a single run — the exclusion would be reached
-- before any provider call and the run would terminate as a HarnessFailure
-- every time. So for those seven a dispatch-time check is unreachable by
-- construction: the load-time rejection fires first, and always.
--
-- `on_tool_handle` is the ONE gated slot (`contains_tool`, ext/runtime.ail:356).
-- It is therefore the only slot whose dispatch can be reached-or-not at runtime,
-- which makes it the only slot where a dispatch-time check can say something a
-- load-time check cannot. `dst_profile.is_excluded_hook`'s header has said this
-- since WI-A10; this module is where it becomes executable.
--
-- ============================================================================
-- WHAT IS VACUOUS TODAY, SAID PLAINLY
-- ============================================================================
--
-- **No profile in this tree can currently reach this guard, and the reason is
-- the same one that blocks the compose install.** `driver_only` installs
-- nothing, so its disclosure list is empty and `is_excluded_hook` is false for
-- every argument. A profile that installed an extension and excluded its
-- `on_tool_handle` would reach it — but no extension in this tree is
-- installable while `ExtensionHooks.on_budget_plan` carries the ABI's closed
-- `! {Env, FS}` row and returns a `BudgetPatch` with no successor field
-- (`packages/motoko-ext-abi/types.ail:298`).
--
-- SUPERSEDED IN ITS SECOND HALF AT WI-D6 (2026-08-06); the CONCLUSION stands
-- and the REASON does not. Restated in two parts per WI-D2's rule, so the next
-- reader can see which half is load-bearing:
--
--   STILL TRUE: no profile in this tree reaches this guard. `driver_only`
--   installs nothing, so its disclosure list is empty and `is_excluded_hook`
--   is false for every argument. The fixture below is still synthetic and
--   these rows still establish the MECHANISM rather than a protected profile.
--
--   NO LONGER TRUE: the clause about `on_budget_plan`. WI-D6 narrowed that row
--   to none after measuring all fifteen bindings, so that particular slot no
--   longer refuses anything.
--
-- CORRECTED AT WI-D7 (2026-08-06), AND THE CORRECTION IS TO WHAT WI-D6 WROTE
-- HERE RATHER THAN TO WHAT WI-D6 DID. The paragraph above used to continue
-- "so an extension IS now installable in a conformant profile", and that does
-- not follow. `on_budget_plan` was ONE of FOUR unconditionally-dispatched
-- slots, and D5 forbids installing an extension with ANY of them excluded, so
-- every one has to be coverable before any extension is installable.
--
-- WI-D7 measured the other three across all fifteen bindings. All three still
-- declare non-empty rows, so all three still fail criterion 1, and NO
-- EXTENSION IS INSTALLABLE. The count is derived on every run by
-- `make profile_definition` and printed as a number rather than as a claim.
--
-- So the honest version of the sentence is: this guard is unreached for TWO
-- reasons still — `driver_only` installs nothing, AND nothing is installable
-- anyway. WI-D6 removed one of four barriers; it did not remove the class.
--
-- So the guard is STRUCTURE, not enforcement-in-effect, and the acceptance
-- script drives it with a SYNTHETIC definition that does exclude a gated hook.
-- That is a real exercise of the mechanism and it is not a claim that any
-- shipping profile is protected by it. Recorded here rather than left for a
-- reader to discover from a green.

module src/core/dst_hook_guard

import std/option (Option, Some, None)
import std/result (Result, Ok, Err)

import src/core/dst_profile (ProfileDefinition, routing_violation_at)
import src/core/dst_result (DstResult, ReplayMetadata)
import src/core/phase_vocab (LedgerTrace)
import src/core/tool_contract (ToolCallEnvelope)
import src/core/ext/runtime as Rt (dispatch_tool_handle)

import pkg/sunholo/motoko_ext_abi/types (
  ExtCtx, ExtRuntime, ExtEntry, Capability, ToolProvider, ToolHandleOutcome
)

-- The dispatch predicate, mirrored from `ext/runtime.first_handle`.
--
-- IT IS A MIRROR AND THAT IS A LIABILITY, stated because the alternative is to
-- pretend otherwise: if `first_handle`'s gate ever stops being
-- `contains_tool(h.provided_tools, name)`, this guard silently starts checking
-- a different set of hooks than the fold dispatches — fail-OPEN, in the
-- direction that grants coverage credit. The acceptance script pins the two
-- together by driving a case where the gate is false and asserting the guard
-- stays silent; that is the only thing standing between them.
pure func provides_tool(xs: [string], name: string) -> bool {
  match xs {
    [] => false,
    x :: rest => if x == name then true else provides_tool(rest, name)
  }
}

-- The first hook that WOULD be dispatched for this call and is excluded.
--
-- Order matters and matches the fold's: `first_handle` walks the registry in
-- order and stops at the first hook that both provides the tool and returns
-- `Handled`. The guard reports the first hook that would be INVOKED, because
-- that is the one whose dispatch is reached.
-- B8: the index-within-kind of the FIRST ToolProvider atom of the entry that
-- advertises `name` (D3: names are disjoint within an extension, so at most
-- one), or -1. The guard names that atom, `tool_provider[i]`, to the profile.
pure func providing_atom(caps: [Capability], name: string, i: int) -> int {
  match caps {
    [] => -1,
    ToolProvider(names, _) :: rest => if provides_tool(names, name) then i else providing_atom(rest, name, i + 1),
    _ :: rest => providing_atom(rest, name, i)
  }
}

pure func first_excluded_for_call(
  d: ProfileDefinition,
  hooks: [ExtEntry],
  name: string,
  position: int,
  partial: LedgerTrace,
  meta: ReplayMetadata
) -> Option[DstResult] {
  match hooks {
    [] => None,
    h :: rest => {
      let i = providing_atom(h.caps, name, 0);
      if i >= 0 then
        match routing_violation_at(d, h.id, "tool_provider[${show(i)}]", position, partial, meta) {
          Some(v) => Some(v),
          None => first_excluded_for_call(d, rest, name, position, partial, meta)
        }
      else first_excluded_for_call(d, rest, name, position, partial, meta)
    }
  }
}

-- The guarded dispatch. `Err` carries the typed `RunFailed` with the partial
-- ledger trace and the interaction position, which is D6.6's distinction from a
-- raw capability bypass: an exclusion violation is observed by the runner while
-- the driver is still running, so it returns a VALUE. A capability bypass
-- terminates evaluation and can return nothing at all.
--
-- `position` is the interaction position at the moment of dispatch, so the
-- failure is locatable in the program that produced it. It is deliberately NOT
-- -1, which D6.7 reserves for a failure predating any interaction — and a
-- dispatch-time violation by definition has interactions behind it.
export func guarded_dispatch_tool_handle(
  d: ProfileDefinition,
  rt: ExtRuntime,
  ctx: ExtCtx,
  call: ToolCallEnvelope,
  position: int,
  partial: LedgerTrace,
  meta: ReplayMetadata
) -> Result[ToolHandleOutcome, DstResult] ! {IO, Process, FS, AI, Env, Net, SharedMem, Clock, Stream, Rand} {
  match first_excluded_for_call(d, rt.registry.entries, call.tool, position, partial, meta) {
    -- Checked BEFORE the fold runs. A guard that dispatched first and inspected
    -- afterwards would have let the excluded hook perform its effects, which is
    -- the whole thing D5 is preventing.
    Some(violation) => Err(violation),
    None => Ok(Rt.dispatch_tool_handle(rt, ctx, call))
  }
}
